---
const editorNative = [
  {
    label: "One-keystroke context (Ctrl+Alt+A)",
    body: "Hand your active selection or open file directly to the agent — no manual copy-pasting or explaining line numbers.",
  },
  {
    label: "Native diff review & instant Undo",
    body: "Inspect proposed changes inside Sublime's own diff view (Accept / Reject) and revert any agent edit in one shortcut (Ctrl+Alt+U).",
  },
  {
    label: "Granular approval boundaries",
    body: "Let file reads and codebase searches run freely while writes, shell commands, web lookups, and MCP tools ask for your green light.",
  },
  {
    label: "Automatic LSP error feedback",
    body: "When an edit introduces a syntax or type issue, Sublime's LSP reports it back so the agent can fix its own mistake immediately.",
  },
  {
    label: "Four task-focused modes",
    body: "Switch between Ask (read-only), Architect (planning), Code (implementation), and Debug so the agent only uses the tools the step requires.",
  },
  {
    label: "Live token & cost visibility",
    body: "See your active provider, model, reasoning effort, context usage, and session cost right in the status bar — with optional budget alerts.",
  },
];
---

<section id="why-limitcode" class="why-lc">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="section-num reveal">03 — Why Limitcode</span>
      <h2 class="reveal d1">Why not just run Claude Code, Codex, Pi or OpenCode in a terminal?</h2>
      <p class="reveal d2">
        Terminal agents are great if you prefer a shell-driven workflow. Limitcode is built for developers who think and navigate inside their editor — and want the agent right next to their code.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="why-grid">
      <article class="why-card reveal d1">
        <h3>Keep your terminal agents if you love the shell.</h3>
        <p>
          Running Claude Code, Codex, Pi, or OpenCode in a terminal window is a solid workflow. Limitcode isn't here to force you out of tools that already work for you.
        </p>
        <p>
          Limitcode exists for the moments you want to stay in Sublime Text: reading files on disk, reviewing diffs side-by-side, sending a highlighted function in one keystroke, and running approved commands without context-switching to a separate terminal loop. Many buyers use Limitcode as their everyday default inside Sublime and keep CLI agents for heavy shell tasks.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="why-card reveal d2">
        <h3>A true Sublime citizen — not a wrapped terminal.</h3>
        <p>
          Because Limitcode is a native Sublime Text view rather than an embedded shell, it connects directly to how you already edit code:
        </p>
        <ul class="why-list">
          {editorNative.map((item) => (
            <li>
              <strong>{item.label}.</strong> {item.body}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .why-lc {
    padding-top: clamp(56px, 7vw, 96px);
    padding-bottom: clamp(56px, 7vw, 96px);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }

  .why-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 18px;
    margin-top: clamp(36px, 5vw, 60px);
  }

  .why-card {
    padding: clamp(26px, 3.5vw, 40px);
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    background: var(--paper-deep);
  }

  .why-card h3 {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    max-width: 24ch;
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  .why-card p {
    max-width: 52ch;
  }

  .why-card p + p {
    margin-top: 12px;
  }

  .why-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin-top: 18px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  .why-list li {
    padding: 12px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline-soft);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .why-list li strong {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .why-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
